Changed here, in SKILL.md and nonstop.sh:
- Each session turns nonstop mode on and off with its own flag file,
  nonstop-$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.active. Upstream used shared nonstop.activate and
  nonstop.deactivate files, and the next session to stop took them, so one session
  could take another session's activation. The hook ignores those files. Turning
  nonstop on resets the nudge count of the session, and each turn-off command
  removes the count with the flag.
- The hook checks the session ID in Python and ignores it if it has characters other
  than letters, digits, - and _. The shell reads the fields without eval. Python runs
  with -I -S, so it loads no module from the session's directory and no startup code
  from a virtualenv on PATH.
- SKILL.md has a <SUBAGENT-STOP> block, so a subagent does not turn nonstop mode on.
To update: review upstream's diff since that commit, apply it to these files, keep the
changes above, run tests/nonstop.sh, and update this note.

nonstop.sh is a Stop hook. Register it in ~/.claude/settings.json under hooks.Stop as:
  f="$HOME/.claude/skills/nonstop/nonstop.sh"; [ ! -f "$f" ] || bash "$f"
so a machine without this skill installed skips it instead of erroring. It keeps its state
in ~/.claude/hooks/state.
